atriot  and he's a founding member of the young  avengers  we've already had plenty of setups for  the young avengers coming in the mcu  and the fact that eli has been  introduced to the mcu is more evidence  that the young avengers are coming  and soon sam goes and he talks to isaiah  and he learns  the truth the tragic story that is  pretty much straight from marvel comics  the story of how they took 300 black men  and injected them with super soldier  serum not telling them what it really  was  the serum killed pretty much all of the  men except for a handful of them  isaiah explains that some of the  survivors of the serum were then taken  hostage  and he heard them say that they were  going to blow up the prisoner of  war camp where they were kept to get rid  of the evidence of the super soldiers so  isaiah went out of his facility  broke into the prisoner of war camp and  rescued the men  this is something that he does in the  comics as well but in the comics he  actually takes captain america shield  and puts on the uniform and then goes to  save them and that's actually why they  put him in jail in the comics because he  stole the shield in uniform  but in this series they put him in jail  and they experiment on him for  30 years because as he explains  everybody but him even the people that  he rescued started to die from the super  soldier serum until it was just him  so they experimented on him to figure  out why he survived  why the serum worked for him they told  his wife that he was dead  and when he finally escaped thanks to  the help of a nurse who took pity on him  they erased him meaning they took his  dame and everything that he did out of  every single database and records that  they have on file  isaiah bradley does not exist and isaiah  actually prefers this at this point  because he believes that if people know  that he's alive  they're just going to kill him now  isaiah's story essentially makes sam  question if he should even pick up the  sh